1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
 - So one of the two ways that we markup data is XML,

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,560
 the other JSON, first we'll talk about XML.

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,280
 We'll talk about XML more, for a longer time

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,040
 than we talk about JSON.

5
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:14,340
 XML stands for Extensible Markup Language.

6
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:16,940
 There was a number of markup languages in the 90s

7
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:20,540
 that were out there, ways to send data between computers,

8
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:24,220
 and none of them was like amazingly better than the other,

9
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:28,600
 but in the late 1990s, early 1990s, as HTML came out,

10
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,520
 the idea that we could use less thans and greater thans,

11
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:35,680
 you know, or angle brackets, some people call them.

12
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,580
 Once HTML made angle brackets popular

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:41,720
 as a representation format, it was pretty natural

14
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:44,080
 that we would find a data representation format

15
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:47,460
 that would take a similar approach.

16
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:50,040
 So inside XML, we're going to talk about tags,

17
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,080
 we're going to talk about attributes,

18
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,060
 we're going to talk about data,

19
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,320
 and we've already talked about serialization

20
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:55,740
 and deserialization.

21
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:58,400
 Serialization is the act of taking data inside of a computer

22
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,500
 in one programming language, setting it up for transport,

23
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,660
 transporting it across, and then taking it back apart

24
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,340
 and turning it back into the data,

25
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,400
 in whatever internal data it needs to be

26
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,580
 in the destination system.

27
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:13,860
 So here's some basic XML, so we can take a look

28
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,780
 at the various things that make up the XML.

29
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:19,420
 So it's very much like HTML in that we have tags,

30
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:20,260
 less than greater than.

31
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:22,680
 The difference is we get to name the tags anything we want

32
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:26,500
 rather than the A tag or the P tag or the H1 tag,

33
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:28,340
 and there's a beginning tag and an ending tag,

34
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:29,360
 and they're bracketed together,

35
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,660
 and there's syntax errors in XML.

36
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,840
 Syntax errors in XML are more severe

37
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,360
 than syntax errors in HTML.

38
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:39,760
 It's supposed to be right, and if you send bad XML,

39
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:43,440
 it's likely that the far end will not understand it.

40
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,620
 So we have a beginning tag and ending tag,

41
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:47,060
 and so like name and slash name

42
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:48,660
 are a beginning and ending pair.

43
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:50,920
 Then there is the actual textual content,

44
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:53,560
 and that is the material between it.

45
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:55,040
 And then here's a phone and slash phone,

46
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:57,000
 and we have this thing called the attribute.

47
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,920
 The key equals value.

48
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,240
 The key doesn't have double quotes,

49
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,820
 the value always has double quotes,

50
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:05,240
 and this is like href= on an anchor tag,

51
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,140
 and sometimes you have what's called a self-closing tag

52
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,000
 where you don't actually have a closing tag.

53
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,240
 You have all the data that you need in the attributes,

54
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,880
 and so you don't even bother putting an empty text area

55
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,360
 in in a closing tag.

56
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:21,060
 So that is a start tag, an end tag,

57
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:23,160
 attribute, and then a self-closing tag.

58
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:25,660
 Those are some basics of XML.

59
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:30,760
 In general, XML doesn't care too much about whitespace.

60
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:34,540
 It does in the text areas, so in here it matters,

61
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:37,000
 and here it matters, but things like we can indent this

62
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,400
 a little bit differently, and we tend to indent it

63
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,320
 in a way to make it look reasonable,

64
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,420
 although once you have programs sending it back and forth,

65
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:46,160
 they tend to send it more compacted

66
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,580
 just for efficiency purposes.

67
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:54,180
 So one of the concepts is that there is a hierarchical

68
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,440
 structure within an XML document,

69
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,740
 and there are parent nodes and child nodes,

70
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:01,240
 and you can think of these as simple nodes

71
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:05,460
 that is a tag in some data, or a complex element

72
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:09,920
 that has a tag that includes other tags, some child tags.

73
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,000
 And there's a couple of different ways

74
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,760
 we can take a look at this.

75
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,320
 The simple and more natural way to think about this

76
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,160
 is a tree with parent-child relationships.

77
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,440
 So here we have this A tag on the outside,

78
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:23,240
 and that's the top level one.

79
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:25,320
 You can only have one outer tag,

80
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:28,420
 and you can't have another tag down here.

81
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,020
 So you have to have one tag that's sort of the root tag

82
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:33,560
 for everything in this XML document,

83
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,920
 and it has two children, so the C tag and the B tag

84
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:40,560
 are two children, so the B tag is a child of A,

85
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:44,520
 and then C has a D and an E tag that are children there,

86
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:48,360
 and then the textual data we model as a child

87
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:52,280
 of each of those tags, and you'll see in a bit

88
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:54,280
 why it's best to do that.

89
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:58,000
 So that is the way to think about this as a tree,

90
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,200
 to represent that XML as a tree.

91
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:03,840
 If we add attributes to it, and this is where you kind of see

92
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:06,240
 why it's nice to take the text area

93
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:08,040
 and make that be a child of the node,

94
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:09,840
 an attribute is a different.

95
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:12,600
 So the text is a special kind of child,

96
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:14,440
 and you can literally have more than one attribute.

97
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:19,080
 You could have x equals two, you know, zap equals whatever,

98
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,960
 and these could have a couple of different attributes.

99
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,760
 The W attribute is a value of five,

100
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:26,360
 and that's the five down there,

101
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:27,640
 and so you could have multiple ones.

102
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:29,760
 You can only have one text node.

103
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,520
 Now, in the case of A, you have a whole bunch of text nodes,

104
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:35,240
 but these are because they're child nodes.

105
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:39,360
 Within one simple node, you can only have one text element.

106
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,680
 You can also think of XML as paths,

107
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:44,080
 and the easiest way is to sort of look down

108
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,560
 this tree version and look at from the path from the parent.

109
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:50,400
 So you go to A, then the child B, and then X.

110
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:53,600
 So at position A, B, you find X.

111
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,560
 So A, B is the path up to the root.

112
00:04:56,560 --> 00:05:01,280
 So A, C, D, that's this one, is the path to Y,

113
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:04,200
 and A, C, E is the path to Z,

114
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,360
 and so you can think of these as paths.

115
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:08,360
 Part of what we're doing is we're coming up with ways

116
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:12,200
 to walk through and parse trees of XML data.

117
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:16,440
 So the next thing we'll talk about is how we determine

118
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:21,440
 if a particular XML document is legal or meets the contracts

119
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,240
 that two applications have set up.

120
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:28,300
 (gentle music)

